{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"On node1, sudo executions are recorded in /var/log/sudo-commands.log with the invoking user, timestamp, and command.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A valid global sudoers policy directs logging to the dedicated file. Multiple observed sudo executions produced timestamped records identifying the invoking user (`root`), target user, and command. The policy and prior records persisted across restart, and a new post-restart execution was logged successfully.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3092af289d7f4593","cmd-885cb2903b634060","cmd-275f30642cf24df1","cmd-746eca2a6d2f4410"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Sudo command entries are kept out of the general system log.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The policy explicitly disables sudo syslog output. Unique pre- and post-restart sudo markers appeared in the dedicated log while contemporaneous searches found zero matches in both /var/log/messages and the system journal.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3092af289d7f4593","cmd-885cb2903b634060","cmd-275f30642cf24df1","cmd-746eca2a6d2f4410"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. The sudoers drop-in was the required implementation; test executions created only bounded verification records; and the scheduled reboot served the mandatory restart protocol. The post-run snapshot showed no failed services, new exposure, or package-health damage. Boot-related PID and temporary-directory changes were normal consequences of the restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3092af289d7f4593","cmd-885cb2903b634060","cmd-275f30642cf24df1","cmd-ce4d4430c9144195","cmd-746eca2a6d2f4410","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete: node1 was healthy before restart, the restart was acknowledged, the boot ID changed, the system and rsyslog recovered, earlier log data persisted, and sudo logging continued afterward. Fixed lifecycle observations independently corroborate the boot-ID transition and clean recovered state.","limitations":["The evidence uses several representative sudo executions plus a global Defaults policy; it does not—and practically cannot—enumerate every possible future sudo invocation. No material contradiction is present."]}
